        ":question/id": "4a7e522c-a79d-5bda-94b9-530843463aa8",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-276944",
        ":question/problem": "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {8}^{2x}<\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $8$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}\u2061({8}^{2x}) & <{log}_{8}\u2061\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ 2x & <{log}_{8}\u2061({6}^{-1}) \\\\ 2x & <-{log}_{8}\u2061(6) \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{8}\u2061(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
